i was thinking about data filters, compost shims.
 <% shim Yaml $yamldata %> ... <% /shim %>

brings that parsed yaml data structure into the global hash,
till the end tag.

More verbosely
 <% shim Yaml -file $file -namespace shim %> ... <% /shim %>

parses $file and roots the namespace in 'shim'..
  <% $shim.config %>

also, the pulling in shimed data as a template object method
  $template->set_shim( 'Yaml', $data );

there could be shims for...
  DBI, XML, Yaml, Bdb, CSV,
  splitting stdin on columns/fields,
  splitting stdin on simple matches,
  splitting log files, 

parser should catch errors like <$ switch %>
and nonexistant parameters,

report em early - with line numbers

# thinking out loud...

you've got a global namespace
 supplied by code, it's the default namespace.
 we don't want it to be changed by feeding it to Compost.

and a state namespace, also supplied by code,
 it's key value and the values can be grepped.

if the best way to solve the inner loop namespace bug,
 is to create a template working namespace.

this raises two issues, symbols and assignment.

we could go the perl route..
  $var   : the global var
  @var   : private vars like in ruby
  %state : is like <% %>, it still does control stuff


questions:
  do they all interpolate in strings,
    and therefore must be escaped?  - would say no
  can @var use nested namespace?
    ie, can we <% loop @var %>

or go the keyword way

currently state is <% state Foo page.* %>
 which uses a keyword, and asks
  if $state.Foo =~ /^page\.\w.*/

private vars may use
  <% my $foo = $bar %>
hmmmm

so assignment to...
   $var   - not allowed
   @var    - allowed (put loop vars here, @FIRST @EVEN @COUNT)
   %state  - allowed, must be /^\w+(\.\w+)*$/

it doesn't solve our current namespace issue,
so we need a way to refer to global, current,
previous, previous previous, ...

we currently specify global namespace root by
 <% $foo -global %>
we could specify previous by..
 <% $foo -prev %>

Or we could use symbols
  $var     - current namespace
  $.var    - rooted in global namespace (removes need for -global)
  $..var   - up one nesting
  $...var  - up two nestings

this solves many of the above issues,
and doesn't require assignment.

unless you wanna add it...
  $:var (put loop vars here, $:FIRST $:EVEN $:COUNT)
  <% $:var = $var %>

hmmmm
  <% $:var = [ 5, 4, 3, 2 ] %>  it creates an arrayref
  <% $:var = { foo => $bar } %> creates a hashref
yucky

